# Every var must be VITE_-prefixed to reach the browser (Vite's rule).
# Copy to `.env.local` and override only what your setup needs — every one of
# these has a working default, so an empty file is a valid configuration.

# Where the dev server proxies /api, /ws and /media. The Django dev server, by
# default. Point it elsewhere when the backend runs in Docker or on another
# machine on the LAN.
VITE_PROXY_TARGET=http://127.0.0.1:8000

# Base origin the API client prepends before /api/v1. Empty means "same origin",
# which is what both the dev proxy and the deployed nginx actually are. Set it
# only when the frontend is served from somewhere other than the thing proxying
# the API — and then CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS on the backend has to name this app.
# VITE_API_URL=

# Base origin for the realtime WebSocket. Same rule, same default. The *scheme*
# is never configured — it is derived from the page's own, so an https page
# cannot be talked into an insecure socket by a stale value here.
# VITE_WS_URL=

# Google OAuth client id. Unset hides the Google button — which is also what
# happens when the *server* has no credentials (GET /auth/config/ reports it).
# Both halves have to be present for the button to be worth showing.
# VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# --- Pictures of server-side numbers -----------------------------------------
# These do not change any behaviour: the server holds the clock and the grace
# period, and it is the only side that decides when either has run out. They
# exist so a deployment that retunes the backend can retune the countdown that
# draws it, without a rebuild. Keep them in step with
# apps/matches/constants.py — QUESTION_TIME_LIMIT_SECONDS and
# RECONNECT_GRACE_SECONDS — or the bar will disagree with the game.
# VITE_QUESTION_TIME_LIMIT_MS=10000
# VITE_RECONNECT_GRACE_MS=20000
